      {"bad":"The family plans its holidays carefully. They hope visiting the coast this year. The children are excited about the trip. They look forward to swim in the sea. The hotel was booked in March.","good":"The family plans its holidays carefully. They hope to visit the coast this year. The children are excited about the trip. They look forward to swimming in the sea. The hotel was booked in March.","explanation":"\"Hope\" takes an infinitive; after a preposition use the gerund \"swimming\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The family plans its holidays carefully.","good":"The family plans its holidays carefully."},{"bad":"They hope visiting the coast this year.","good":"They hope to visit the coast this year."},{"bad":"The children are excited about the trip.","good":"The children are excited about the trip."},{"bad":"They look forward to swim in the sea.","good":"They look forward to swimming in the sea."},{"bad":"The hotel was booked in March.","good":"The hotel was booked in March."}]},
      {"bad":"Ben has started a new hobby. He has begun to collects old stamps. He keeps them in a neat album. He also enjoys to read about history. His collection is growing quickly.","good":"Ben has started a new hobby. He has begun to collect old stamps. He keeps them in a neat album. He also enjoys reading about history. His collection is growing quickly.","explanation":"\"Begin\" takes an infinitive (\"to collect\"); \"enjoy\" takes a gerund (\"reading\").","sentences":[{"bad":"Ben has started a new hobby.","good":"Ben has started a new hobby."},{"bad":"He has begun to collects old stamps.","good":"He has begun to collect old stamps."},{"bad":"He keeps them in a neat album.","good":"He keeps them in a neat album."},{"bad":"He also enjoys to read about history.","good":"He also enjoys reading about history."},{"bad":"His collection is growing quickly.","good":"His collection is growing quickly."}]},
      {"bad":"The couple planned their new kitchen. They wanted choosing the tiles themselves. They visited several shops. They finished to paint the walls on Sunday. The kitchen looked lovely in the end.","good":"The couple planned their new kitchen. They wanted to choose the tiles themselves. They visited several shops. They finished painting the walls on Sunday. The kitchen looked lovely in the end.","explanation":"\"Want\" takes an infinitive; \"finish\" takes a gerund.","sentences":[{"bad":"The couple planned their new kitchen.","good":"The couple planned their new kitchen."},{"bad":"They wanted choosing the tiles themselves.","good":"They wanted to choose the tiles themselves."},{"bad":"They visited several shops.","good":"They visited several shops."},{"bad":"They finished to paint the walls on Sunday.","good":"They finished painting the walls on Sunday."},{"bad":"The kitchen looked lovely in the end.","good":"The kitchen looked lovely in the end."}]},
      {"bad":"The students prepared for the exam. They avoided to study the hardest topic. The teacher set extra practice questions. Everyone agreed working together more often. Their results improved a great deal.","good":"The students prepared for the exam. They avoided studying the hardest topic. The teacher set extra practice questions. Everyone agreed to work together more often. Their results improved a great deal.","explanation":"\"Avoid\" takes a gerund; \"agree\" takes an infinitive.","sentences":[{"bad":"The students prepared for the exam.","good":"The students prepared for the exam."},{"bad":"They avoided to study the hardest topic.","good":"They avoided studying the hardest topic."},{"bad":"The teacher set extra practice questions.","good":"The teacher set extra practice questions."},{"bad":"Everyone agreed working together more often.","good":"Everyone agreed to work together more often."},{"bad":"Their results improved a great deal.","good":"Their results improved a great deal."}]}
