    "9.3": [
      {"bad":"The class planned a group project. Both the teacher and the pupils was excited. They divided the work fairly. The news about the topic are surprising. The project took two weeks.","good":"The class planned a group project. Both the teacher and the pupils were excited. They divided the work fairly. The news about the topic is surprising. The project took two weeks.","explanation":"A compound subject takes \"were\"; \"news\" is singular, so \"is\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The class planned a group project.","good":"The class planned a group project."},{"bad":"Both the teacher and the pupils was excited.","good":"Both the teacher and the pupils were excited."},{"bad":"They divided the work fairly.","good":"They divided the work fairly."},{"bad":"The news about the topic are surprising.","good":"The news about the topic is surprising."},{"bad":"The project took two weeks.","good":"The project took two weeks."}]},
      {"bad":"The family runs a small farm. The family are gathering the harvest today. Everyone works from dawn. Neither the dogs nor the cat were inside. The farm is always busy.","good":"The family runs a small farm. The family is gathering the harvest today. Everyone works from dawn. Neither the dogs nor the cat was inside. The farm is always busy.","explanation":"Treat \"family\" as one unit here: \"is gathering\"; with \"nor\", the nearer subject controls: \"cat was\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The family runs a small farm.","good":"The family runs a small farm."},{"bad":"The family are gathering the harvest today.","good":"The family is gathering the harvest today."},{"bad":"Everyone works from dawn.","good":"Everyone works from dawn."},{"bad":"Neither the dogs nor the cat were inside.","good":"Neither the dogs nor the cat was inside."},{"bad":"The farm is always busy.","good":"The farm is always busy."}]},
      {"bad":"The club meets on Thursdays. Either the coach or the players is responsible. They review the rules each week. Mathematics are my favourite subject. The club has many members.","good":"The club meets on Thursdays. Either the coach or the players are responsible. They review the rules each week. Mathematics is my favourite subject. The club has many members.","explanation":"With \"either... or\", the nearer subject controls: \"players are\"; \"mathematics\" is singular.","sentences":[{"bad":"The club meets on Thursdays.","good":"The club meets on Thursdays."},{"bad":"Either the coach or the players is responsible.","good":"Either the coach or the players are responsible."},{"bad":"They review the rules each week.","good":"They review the rules each week."},{"bad":"Mathematics are my favourite subject.","good":"Mathematics is my favourite subject."},{"bad":"The club has many members.","good":"The club has many members."}]},
      {"bad":"The company opened a new branch. Both the manager and the staff has moved. The building is bright and modern. The team has finished their training. The branch opens next week.","good":"The company opened a new branch. Both the manager and the staff have moved. The building is bright and modern. The team has finished its training. The branch opens next week.","explanation":"Use \"have moved\" for the compound subject and \"its\" for the collective noun.","sentences":[{"bad":"The company opened a new branch.","good":"The company opened a new branch."},{"bad":"Both the manager and the staff has moved.","good":"Both the manager and the staff have moved."},{"bad":"The building is bright and modern.","good":"The building is bright and modern."},{"bad":"The team has finished their training.","good":"The team has finished its training."},{"bad":"The branch opens next week.","good":"The branch opens next week."}]},
      {"bad":"The village prepares for the festival. The committee have made its decision. Decorations appear on every street. Neither the mayor nor the villagers was absent. The festival lasts three days.","good":"The village prepares for the festival. The committee has made its decision. Decorations appear on every street. Neither the mayor nor the villagers were absent. The festival lasts three days.","explanation":"Use \"committee has\" and, with \"nor\", match the nearer subject \"villagers were\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The village prepares for the festival.","good":"The village prepares for the festival."},{"bad":"The committee have made its decision.","good":"The committee has made its decision."},{"bad":"Decorations appear on every street.","good":"Decorations appear on every street."},{"bad":"Neither the mayor nor the villagers was absent.","good":"Neither the mayor nor the villagers were absent."},{"bad":"The festival lasts three days.","good":"The festival lasts three days."}]}
    ],
    "9.4": [
      {"bad":"The company updated its website. The company has changed their logo. Customers noticed the new look at once. Each department printed their own leaflets. The new brand looks modern.","good":"The company updated its website. The company has changed its logo. Customers noticed the new look at once. Each department printed its own leaflets. The new brand looks modern.","explanation":"A singular noun like \"company\" or \"each department\" takes \"its\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The company updated its website.","good":"The company updated its website."},{"bad":"The company has changed their logo.","good":"The company has changed its logo."},{"bad":"Customers noticed the new look at once.","good":"Customers noticed the new look at once."},{"bad":"Each department printed their own leaflets.","good":"Each department printed its own leaflets."},{"bad":"The new brand looks modern.","good":"The new brand looks modern."}]},
      {"bad":"The dog guarded the gate. Every dog wagged their tail. The postman arrived at noon. Someone left their bag by the door. The gate stayed closed.","good":"The dog guarded the gate. Every dog wagged its tail. The postman arrived at noon. Someone left his bag by the door. The gate stayed closed.","explanation":"Singular antecedents (\"every dog\", \"someone\") take singular pronouns.","sentences":[{"bad":"The dog guarded the gate.","good":"The dog guarded the gate."},{"bad":"Every dog wagged their tail.","good":"Every dog wagged its tail."},{"bad":"The postman arrived at noon.","good":"The postman arrived at noon."},{"bad":"Someone left their bag by the door.","good":"Someone left his bag by the door."},{"bad":"The gate stayed closed.","good":"The gate stayed closed."}]},
      {"bad":"The students finished the project. Each pupil chose their topic carefully. They presented the work on Friday. Neither girl had brought their notes. The teacher praised the effort.","good":"The students finished the project. Each pupil chose a topic carefully. They presented the work on Friday. Neither girl had brought her notes. The teacher praised the effort.","explanation":"\"Each\" and \"neither\" are singular: \"her notes\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The students finished the project.","good":"The students finished the project."},{"bad":"Each pupil chose their topic carefully.","good":"Each pupil chose a topic carefully."},{"bad":"They presented the work on Friday.","good":"They presented the work on Friday."},{"bad":"Neither girl had brought their notes.","good":"Neither girl had brought her notes."},{"bad":"The teacher praised the effort.","good":"The teacher praised the effort."}]},
      {"bad":"The choir prepared for the concert. Every singer tuned their voice. The hall filled with sound. The audience clapped their hands. The concert was a triumph.","good":"The choir prepared for the concert. Every singer tuned his voice. The hall filled with sound. The audience clapped their hands. The concert was a triumph.","explanation":"With \"every singer\" use a singular possessive; \"the audience\" may take \"their\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The choir prepared for the concert.","good":"The choir prepared for the concert."},{"bad":"Every singer tuned their voice.","good":"Every singer tuned his voice."},{"bad":"The hall filled with sound.","good":"The hall filled with sound."},{"bad":"The audience clapped their hands.","good":"The audience clapped their hands."},{"bad":"The concert was a triumph.","good":"The concert was a triumph."}]},
      {"bad":"The business grew very quickly. The firm rewarded their best staff. Morale improved at once. Each team shared their ideas. The company opened a second office.","good":"The business grew very quickly. The firm rewarded its best staff. Morale improved at once. Each team shared its ideas. The company opened a second office.","explanation":"Use \"its\" with the singular \"firm\" and \"each team\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The business grew very quickly.","good":"The business grew very quickly."},{"bad":"The firm rewarded their best staff.","good":"The firm rewarded its best staff."},{"bad":"Morale improved at once.","good":"Morale improved at once."},{"bad":"Each team shared their ideas.","good":"Each team shared its ideas."},{"bad":"The company opened a second office.","good":"The company opened a second office."}]}
    ],
    "9.5": [
      {"bad":"The sky turned grey by noon. It was raining, we stayed inside. We played board games all afternoon. The rain stopped, we went outside at once. The evening was calm and clear.","good":"The sky turned grey by noon. It was raining, so we stayed inside. We played board games all afternoon. The rain stopped, and we went outside at once. The evening was calm and clear.","explanation":"Join two clauses with a conjunction: \"so\", \"and\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The sky turned grey by noon.","good":"The sky turned grey by noon."},{"bad":"It was raining, we stayed inside.","good":"It was raining, so we stayed inside."},{"bad":"We played board games all afternoon.","good":"We played board games all afternoon."},{"bad":"The rain stopped, we went outside at once.","good":"The rain stopped, and we went outside at once."},{"bad":"The evening was calm and clear.","good":"The evening was calm and clear."}]},
      {"bad":"My brother studies every evening. He works hard he wants good marks. His effort shows in his results. The exam is near, he revises daily. He feels ready for it.","good":"My brother studies every evening. He works hard because he wants good marks. His effort shows in his results. The exam is near, so he revises daily. He feels ready for it.","explanation":"A comma splice needs a conjunction: \"because\", \"so\".","sentences":[{"bad":"My brother studies every evening.","good":"My brother studies every evening."},{"bad":"He works hard he wants good marks.","good":"He works hard because he wants good marks."},{"bad":"His effort shows in his results.","good":"His effort shows in his results."},{"bad":"The exam is near, he revises daily.","good":"The exam is near, so he revises daily."},{"bad":"He feels ready for it.","good":"He feels ready for it."}]},
      {"bad":"The team gathered for the match. They warmed up, the coach gave a talk. The whistle blew at three. The play was quick, it was exciting too. The team won by two goals.","good":"The team gathered for the match. They warmed up, and the coach gave a talk. The whistle blew at three. The play was quick, and it was exciting too. The team won by two goals.","explanation":"Use \"and\" to join the clauses instead of a bare comma.","sentences":[{"bad":"The team gathered for the match.","good":"The team gathered for the match."},{"bad":"They warmed up, the coach gave a talk.","good":"They warmed up, and the coach gave a talk."},{"bad":"The whistle blew at three.","good":"The whistle blew at three."},{"bad":"The play was quick, it was exciting too.","good":"The play was quick, and it was exciting too."},{"bad":"The team won by two goals.","good":"The team won by two goals."}]},
      {"bad":"The hikers set out at dawn. The path was steep, they kept climbing. The view from the top was stunning. They rested a while, they ate their lunch. They were home by dusk.","good":"The hikers set out at dawn. The path was steep, but they kept climbing. The view from the top was stunning. They rested a while and ate their lunch. They were home by dusk.","explanation":"Use \"but\" or drop the second subject: \"rested and ate\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The hikers set out at dawn.","good":"The hikers set out at dawn."},{"bad":"The path was steep, they kept climbing.","good":"The path was steep, but they kept climbing."},{"bad":"The view from the top was stunning.","good":"The view from the top was stunning."},{"bad":"They rested a while, they ate their lunch.","good":"They rested a while and ate their lunch."},{"bad":"They were home by dusk.","good":"They were home by dusk."}]},
      {"bad":"The village held its summer fair. The stalls opened, a band played on the green. Children queued for ice cream. It grew dark, the lanterns were lit. The fair ended with fireworks.","good":"The village held its summer fair. The stalls opened, and a band played on the green. Children queued for ice cream. When it grew dark, the lanterns were lit. The fair ended with fireworks.","explanation":"Join the clauses with \"and\" or a subordinator like \"when\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The village held its summer fair.","good":"The village held its summer fair."},{"bad":"The stalls opened, a band played on the green.","good":"The stalls opened, and a band played on the green."},{"bad":"Children queued for ice cream.","good":"Children queued for ice cream."},{"bad":"It grew dark, the lanterns were lit.","good":"When it grew dark, the lanterns were lit."},{"bad":"The fair ended with fireworks.","good":"The fair ended with fireworks."}]}
    ],
    "9.6": [
      {"bad":"We waited at the bus stop. Because the bus was late. The journey took an hour. Although the roads were clear. We were glad to reach home.","good":"We waited at the bus stop. Because the bus was late, we arrived tired. The journey took an hour. Although the roads were clear, the trip felt long. We were glad to reach home.","explanation":"A subordinate clause needs a main clause: \"Because ... we ...\".","sentences":[{"bad":"We waited at the bus stop.","good":"We waited at the bus stop."},{"bad":"Because the bus was late.","good":"Because the bus was late, we arrived tired."},{"bad":"The journey took an hour.","good":"The journey took an hour."},{"bad":"Although the roads were clear.","good":"Although the roads were clear, the trip felt long."},{"bad":"We were glad to reach home.","good":"We were glad to reach home."}]},
      {"bad":"The class prepared for the trip. Which was arranged by the teacher. Everyone brought a packed lunch. Even though it was raining heavily. The day was a great success.","good":"The class prepared for the trip. The trip, which was arranged by the teacher, took place on Friday. Everyone brought a packed lunch. Even though it was raining heavily, we set off on time. The day was a great success.","explanation":"Fragments beginning with \"which\" or \"even though\" must join a main clause.","sentences":[{"bad":"The class prepared for the trip.","good":"The class prepared for the trip."},{"bad":"Which was arranged by the teacher.","good":"The trip, which was arranged by the teacher, took place on Friday."},{"bad":"Everyone brought a packed lunch.","good":"Everyone brought a packed lunch."},{"bad":"Even though it was raining heavily.","good":"Even though it was raining heavily, we set off on time."},{"bad":"The day was a great success.","good":"The day was a great success."}]},
      {"bad":"The puppy explored the garden. Running through the flower beds. Its paws were covered in mud. Barking at the birds in the trees. The garden was a mess afterwards.","good":"The puppy explored the garden. It ran through the flower beds. Its paws were covered in mud. It barked at the birds in the trees. The garden was a mess afterwards.","explanation":"A bare -ing phrase is a fragment; give it a subject and a verb.","sentences":[{"bad":"The puppy explored the garden.","good":"The puppy explored the garden."},{"bad":"Running through the flower beds.","good":"It ran through the flower beds."},{"bad":"Its paws were covered in mud.","good":"Its paws were covered in mud."},{"bad":"Barking at the birds in the trees.","good":"It barked at the birds in the trees."},{"bad":"The garden was a mess afterwards.","good":"The garden was a mess afterwards."}]},
      {"bad":"The shop closed at six. The one on the corner. A note hung on the door. A quiet little place with old shelves. We agreed to return tomorrow.","good":"The shop closed at six. The one on the corner closed early. A note hung on the door. It was a quiet little place with old shelves. We agreed to return tomorrow.","explanation":"A noun phrase alone is a fragment; add a verb.","sentences":[{"bad":"The shop closed at six.","good":"The shop closed at six."},{"bad":"The one on the corner.","good":"The one on the corner closed early."},{"bad":"A note hung on the door.","good":"A note hung on the door."},{"bad":"A quiet little place with old shelves.","good":"It was a quiet little place with old shelves."},{"bad":"We agreed to return tomorrow.","good":"We agreed to return tomorrow."}]},
      {"bad":"The team lost the final. Despite playing very well. They shook hands with the winners. And promised to return next year. The season ended with pride.","good":"The team lost the final. Despite playing very well, they lost the final. They shook hands with the winners. They promised to return next year. The season ended with pride.","explanation":"Attach the phrase to a main clause: \"Despite ... they lost\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The team lost the final.","good":"The team lost the final."},{"bad":"Despite playing very well.","good":"Despite playing very well, they lost the final."},{"bad":"They shook hands with the winners.","good":"They shook hands with the winners."},{"bad":"And promised to return next year.","good":"They promised to return next year."},{"bad":"The season ended with pride.","good":"The season ended with pride."}]}
    ],
    "9.7": [
      {"bad":"I walked home through the park. Walking home, the rain started. I hurried along the path. Having no umbrella, my coat got soaked. I was glad to reach the door.","good":"I walked home through the park. Walking home, I felt the rain start. I hurried along the path. Having no umbrella, I got my coat soaked. I was glad to reach the door.","explanation":"The opening phrase must describe the subject: \"walking home, I ...\".","sentences":[{"bad":"I walked home through the park.","good":"I walked home through the park."},{"bad":"Walking home, the rain started.","good":"Walking home, I felt the rain start."},{"bad":"I hurried along the path.","good":"I hurried along the path."},{"bad":"Having no umbrella, my coat got soaked.","good":"Having no umbrella, I got my coat soaked."},{"bad":"I was glad to reach the door.","good":"I was glad to reach the door."}]},
      {"bad":"The chef prepared the evening meal. Cooked slowly, the chef tasted the sauce. The guests arrived at eight. Being very hungry, the soup smelled wonderful. Everyone enjoyed the dinner.","good":"The chef prepared the evening meal. Cooked slowly, the sauce tasted rich. The guests arrived at eight. Being very hungry, we thought the soup smelled wonderful. Everyone enjoyed the dinner.","explanation":"The modifier must describe the right subject: \"the sauce tasted rich\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The chef prepared the evening meal.","good":"The chef prepared the evening meal."},{"bad":"Cooked slowly, the chef tasted the sauce.","good":"Cooked slowly, the sauce tasted rich."},{"bad":"The guests arrived at eight.","good":"The guests arrived at eight."},{"bad":"Being very hungry, the soup smelled wonderful.","good":"Being very hungry, we thought the soup smelled wonderful."},{"bad":"Everyone enjoyed the dinner.","good":"Everyone enjoyed the dinner."}]},
      {"bad":"The team trained on the field. After training hard, the match was won. The fans cheered them loudly. Exhausted but happy, the trophy was lifted. It was a proud day for the club.","good":"The team trained on the field. After training hard, the team won the match. The fans cheered them loudly. Exhausted but happy, the players lifted the trophy. It was a proud day for the club.","explanation":"Tie the phrase to the doer: \"the team won\", \"the players lifted\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The team trained on the field.","good":"The team trained on the field."},{"bad":"After training hard, the match was won.","good":"After training hard, the team won the match."},{"bad":"The fans cheered them loudly.","good":"The fans cheered them loudly."},{"bad":"Exhausted but happy, the trophy was lifted.","good":"Exhausted but happy, the players lifted the trophy."},{"bad":"It was a proud day for the club.","good":"It was a proud day for the club."}]},
      {"bad":"The students wrote their essays. Having finished the essay, the printer jammed. They handed the work in on time. Writing quickly, some mistakes were made. The teacher marked the essays fairly.","good":"The students wrote their essays. Having finished the essay, the pupils printed it. They handed the work in on time. Writing quickly, they made some mistakes. The teacher marked the essays fairly.","explanation":"The -ing phrase describes the pupils, not the printer or mistakes.","sentences":[{"bad":"The students wrote their essays.","good":"The students wrote their essays."},{"bad":"Having finished the essay, the printer jammed.","good":"Having finished the essay, the pupils printed it."},{"bad":"They handed the work in on time.","good":"They handed the work in on time."},{"bad":"Writing quickly, some mistakes were made.","good":"Writing quickly, they made some mistakes."},{"bad":"The teacher marked the essays fairly.","good":"The teacher marked the essays fairly."}]},
      {"bad":"The scientists studied the ice. Having collected the samples, the results were analysed. The findings were published in June. Working late, the report was finished. The study made the news.","good":"The scientists studied the ice. Having collected the samples, the scientists analysed the results. The findings were published in June. Working late, the team finished the report. The study made the news.","explanation":"The opening phrase must share its subject with the main clause.","sentences":[{"bad":"The scientists studied the ice.","good":"The scientists studied the ice."},{"bad":"Having collected the samples, the results were analysed.","good":"Having collected the samples, the scientists analysed the results."},{"bad":"The findings were published in June.","good":"The findings were published in June."},{"bad":"Working late, the report was finished.","good":"Working late, the team finished the report."},{"bad":"The study made the news.","good":"The study made the news."}]}
    ],
    "9.8": [
      {"bad":"The host welcomed everyone warmly. She served sandwiches to the guests on paper plates. The food was fresh and tasty. She poured tea into the cups with a smile. The party went on all evening.","good":"The host welcomed everyone warmly. She served sandwiches on paper plates to the guests. The food was fresh and tasty. With a smile, she poured tea into the cups. The party went on all evening.","explanation":"Place the modifier next to what it describes: \"on paper plates\" belongs with \"sandwiches\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The host welcomed everyone warmly.","good":"The host welcomed everyone warmly."},{"bad":"She served sandwiches to the guests on paper plates.","good":"She served sandwiches on paper plates to the guests."},{"bad":"The food was fresh and tasty.","good":"The food was fresh and tasty."},{"bad":"She poured tea into the cups with a smile.","good":"With a smile, she poured tea into the cups."},{"bad":"The party went on all evening.","good":"The party went on all evening."}]},
      {"bad":"The gardener tidied the whole yard. She cut the hedge that was overgrown with sharp shears. The lawn looked neat afterwards. She planted roses in the corner that smelled lovely. The garden won a prize.","good":"The gardener tidied the whole yard. With sharp shears, she cut the overgrown hedge. The lawn looked neat afterwards. She planted lovely roses in the corner that smelled nice. The garden won a prize.","explanation":"Keep the modifier close to the word it modifies.","sentences":[{"bad":"The gardener tidied the whole yard.","good":"The gardener tidied the whole yard."},{"bad":"She cut the hedge that was overgrown with sharp shears.","good":"With sharp shears, she cut the overgrown hedge."},{"bad":"The lawn looked neat afterwards.","good":"The lawn looked neat afterwards."},{"bad":"She planted roses in the corner that smelled lovely.","good":"She planted lovely roses in the corner that smelled nice."},{"bad":"The garden won a prize.","good":"The garden won a prize."}]},
      {"bad":"The teacher praised the class. She returned the essays to the pupils with red ink. The marks were very good. She gave a talk about grammar at the front that was useful. The lesson ended with a smile.","good":"The teacher praised the class. She returned the essays with red ink to the pupils. The marks were very good. At the front, she gave a useful talk about grammar. The lesson ended with a smile.","explanation":"Put the modifying phrase next to the noun it belongs to.","sentences":[{"bad":"The teacher praised the class.","good":"The teacher praised the class."},{"bad":"She returned the essays to the pupils with red ink.","good":"She returned the essays with red ink to the pupils."},{"bad":"The marks were very good.","good":"The marks were very good."},{"bad":"She gave a talk about grammar at the front that was useful.","good":"At the front, she gave a useful talk about grammar."},{"bad":"The lesson ended with a smile.","good":"The lesson ended with a smile."}]},
      {"bad":"The mechanic repaired the car. He replaced the brake pads that were worn on the van. The van ran smoothly afterwards. He found a problem with the engine inside the garage. The owner was pleased.","good":"The mechanic repaired the car. He replaced the worn brake pads on the van. The van ran smoothly afterwards. Inside the garage, he found a problem with the engine. The owner was pleased.","explanation":"Place \"worn\" before \"brake pads\" and the location phrase at the start.","sentences":[{"bad":"The mechanic repaired the car.","good":"The mechanic repaired the car."},{"bad":"He replaced the brake pads that were worn on the van.","good":"He replaced the worn brake pads on the van."},{"bad":"The van ran smoothly afterwards.","good":"The van ran smoothly afterwards."},{"bad":"He found a problem with the engine inside the garage.","good":"Inside the garage, he found a problem with the engine."},{"bad":"The owner was pleased.","good":"The owner was pleased."}]},
      {"bad":"The choir sang at the concert. They performed the songs that were new beautifully. The audience listened in silence. The conductor raised his arms at the front with great energy. The concert ended to loud applause.","good":"The choir sang at the concert. They performed the new songs beautifully. The audience listened in silence. The conductor raised his arms with great energy. The concert ended to loud applause.","explanation":"Keep descriptive words next to the nouns they modify.","sentences":[{"bad":"The choir sang at the concert.","good":"The choir sang at the concert."},{"bad":"They performed the songs that were new beautifully.","good":"They performed the new songs beautifully."},{"bad":"The audience listened in silence.","good":"The audience listened in silence."},{"bad":"The conductor raised his arms at the front with great energy.","good":"The conductor raised his arms with great energy."},{"bad":"The concert ended to loud applause.","good":"The concert ended to loud applause."}]}
    ],
    "9.9": [
      {"bad":"She has many hobbies. She likes swimming, running and to ride a bike. She spends her weekends outdoors. Her goals are to stay fit, to have fun and making friends. She is always cheerful.","good":"She has many hobbies. She likes swimming, running and riding a bike. She spends her weekends outdoors. Her goals are to stay fit, to have fun and to make friends. She is always cheerful.","explanation":"Keep items in a list in the same form: all gerunds or all infinitives.","sentences":[{"bad":"She has many hobbies.","good":"She has many hobbies."},{"bad":"She likes swimming, running and to ride a bike.","good":"She likes swimming, running and riding a bike."},{"bad":"She spends her weekends outdoors.","good":"She spends her weekends outdoors."},{"bad":"Her goals are to stay fit, to have fun and making friends.","good":"Her goals are to stay fit, to have fun and to make friends."},{"bad":"She is always cheerful.","good":"She is always cheerful."}]},
      {"bad":"The job requires several skills. It needs patience, care and being reliable. The employer explained the role. The duties are answering calls, filing papers and to greet guests. Many people applied for it.","good":"The job requires several skills. It needs patience, care and reliability. The employer explained the role. The duties are answering calls, filing papers and greeting guests. Many people applied for it.","explanation":"Keep list items parallel: nouns with nouns, gerunds with gerunds.","sentences":[{"bad":"The job requires several skills.","good":"The job requires several skills."},{"bad":"It needs patience, care and being reliable.","good":"It needs patience, care and reliability."},{"bad":"The employer explained the role.","good":"The employer explained the role."},{"bad":"The duties are answering calls, filing papers and to greet guests.","good":"The duties are answering calls, filing papers and greeting guests."},{"bad":"Many people applied for it.","good":"Many people applied for it."}]},
      {"bad":"The town built a new leisure centre. The centre offers swimming, fitness and to join classes. The opening drew large crowds. Members can train, relax or taking a sauna. The centre is open daily.","good":"The town built a new leisure centre. The centre offers swimming, fitness and classes. The opening drew large crowds. Members can train, relax or take a sauna. The centre is open daily.","explanation":"Keep the coordinated items in the same grammatical form.","sentences":[{"bad":"The town built a new leisure centre.","good":"The town built a new leisure centre."},{"bad":"The centre offers swimming, fitness and to join classes.","good":"The centre offers swimming, fitness and classes."},{"bad":"The opening drew large crowds.","good":"The opening drew large crowds."},{"bad":"Members can train, relax or taking a sauna.","good":"Members can train, relax or take a sauna."},{"bad":"The centre is open daily.","good":"The centre is open daily."}]},
      {"bad":"The pupils wrote a report on the trip. The trip was long, tiring but we enjoyed it. They described the mountain views. They saw rivers, forests and some was climbing. The report earned high marks.","good":"The pupils wrote a report on the trip. The trip was long and tiring but enjoyable. They described the mountain views. They saw rivers, forests and mountains. The report earned high marks.","explanation":"Make coordinated adjectives and nouns consistent in form.","sentences":[{"bad":"The pupils wrote a report on the trip.","good":"The pupils wrote a report on the trip."},{"bad":"The trip was long, tiring but we enjoyed it.","good":"The trip was long and tiring but enjoyable."},{"bad":"They described the mountain views.","good":"They described the mountain views."},{"bad":"They saw rivers, forests and some was climbing.","good":"They saw rivers, forests and mountains."},{"bad":"The report earned high marks.","good":"The report earned high marks."}]},
      {"bad":"The manager explained the plan. The plan is simple, clear and it can be done. The staff agreed with it. They will plan, prepare and are going to start soon. The work begins on Monday.","good":"The manager explained the plan. The plan is simple, clear and practical. The staff agreed with it. They will plan, prepare and start soon. The work begins on Monday.","explanation":"Keep coordinated verbs and adjectives parallel.","sentences":[{"bad":"The manager explained the plan.","good":"The manager explained the plan."},{"bad":"The plan is simple, clear and it can be done.","good":"The plan is simple, clear and practical."},{"bad":"The staff agreed with it.","good":"The staff agreed with it."},{"bad":"They will plan, prepare and are going to start soon.","good":"They will plan, prepare and start soon."},{"bad":"The work begins on Monday.","good":"The work begins on Monday."}]}
    ],
    "9.10": [
      {"bad":"The race was long and hard. He was tired, and he finished the race. The crowd cheered him home. The course was tough, and the runners enjoyed it. He was proud of his time.","good":"The race was long and hard. He was tired, but he finished the race. The crowd cheered him home. The course was tough, but the runners enjoyed it. He was proud of his time.","explanation":"Use \"but\" for contrast, \"and\" for addition.","sentences":[{"bad":"The race was long and hard.","good":"The race was long and hard."},{"bad":"He was tired, and he finished the race.","good":"He was tired, but he finished the race."},{"bad":"The crowd cheered him home.","good":"The crowd cheered him home."},{"bad":"The course was tough, and the runners enjoyed it.","good":"The course was tough, but the runners enjoyed it."},{"bad":"He was proud of his time.","good":"He was proud of his time."}]},
      {"bad":"The café serves light meals. The food was cheap, and the service was excellent. It fills up at lunchtime. The queue was long, and the wait was short. We will go back soon.","good":"The café serves light meals. The food was cheap, and the service was excellent. It fills up at lunchtime. The queue was long, but the wait was short. We will go back soon.","explanation":"Choose the conjunction that matches the meaning.","sentences":[{"bad":"The café serves light meals.","good":"The café serves light meals."},{"bad":"The food was cheap, and the service was excellent.","good":"The food was cheap, and the service was excellent."},{"bad":"It fills up at lunchtime.","good":"It fills up at lunchtime."},{"bad":"The queue was long, and the wait was short.","good":"The queue was long, but the wait was short."},{"bad":"We will go back soon.","good":"We will go back soon."}]},
      {"bad":"The team prepared carefully. They practised hard, and they lost the match. The coach praised their effort. The defeat was painful, and it taught them a lot. They trained harder the next week.","good":"The team prepared carefully. They practised hard, but they lost the match. The coach praised their effort. The defeat was painful, but it taught them a lot. They trained harder the next week.","explanation":"Use \"but\" to signal contrast.","sentences":[{"bad":"The team prepared carefully.","good":"The team prepared carefully."},{"bad":"They practised hard, and they lost the match.","good":"They practised hard, but they lost the match."},{"bad":"The coach praised their effort.","good":"The coach praised their effort."},{"bad":"The defeat was painful, and it taught them a lot.","good":"The defeat was painful, but it taught them a lot."},{"bad":"They trained harder the next week.","good":"They trained harder the next week."}]},
      {"bad":"The book was very long. It was heavy, and it was easy to read. I finished it in a week. The story was simple, and the ending surprised me. I lent it to a friend.","good":"The book was very long. It was heavy, but it was easy to read. I finished it in a week. The story was simple, but the ending surprised me. I lent it to a friend.","explanation":"Contrast needs \"but\", not \"and\".","sentences":[{"bad":"The book was very long.","good":"The book was very long."},{"bad":"It was heavy, and it was easy to read.","good":"It was heavy, but it was easy to read."},{"bad":"I finished it in a week.","good":"I finished it in a week."},{"bad":"The story was simple, and the ending surprised me.","good":"The story was simple, but the ending surprised me."},{"bad":"I lent it to a friend.","good":"I lent it to a friend."}]},
      {"bad":"The journey took most of the day. The road was busy, and we arrived on time. We found a place to park. The weather was poor, and the trip was enjoyable. We would do it again.","good":"The journey took most of the day. The road was busy, but we arrived on time. We found a place to park. The weather was poor, but the trip was enjoyable. We would do it again.","explanation":"Use \"but\" when the second clause contrasts with the first.","sentences":[{"bad":"The journey took most of the day.","good":"The journey took most of the day."},{"bad":"The road was busy, and we arrived on time.","good":"The road was busy, but we arrived on time."},{"bad":"We found a place to park.","good":"We found a place to park."},{"bad":"The weather was poor, and the trip was enjoyable.","good":"The weather was poor, but the trip was enjoyable."},{"bad":"We would do it again.","good":"We would do it again."}]}
